# Python solutions for AoC {{ year }}

<!-- What did I want to learn or try this year? -->

Solved with Python {{ python_version }} and the standard library only, plus my own
[`aoc_lib`](../../aoc_lib), a helper library with a Rust extension module.

## Setup

Make sure you have [pixi](https://pixi.sh/latest/#installation) installed. Then run

```shell
pixi install
```

This builds `aoc_lib` from source, so the first run takes a moment.

## Run an example

```shell
pixi run aoc{{ year }} <day> <task> -e <example_id>
```

For example, to run the first example for day 3 task 2:

```shell
pixi run aoc{{ year }} 3 2 -e 1
```

## Run a solution

Leave out `-e <example_id>` to run on the real input:

```shell
pixi run aoc{{ year }} <day> <task>
```

Leave out `<task>` to run both tasks of a day, or `<day>` as well to run every solved day.

## Timing

Add `-t` to benchmark a solution. Each one is run repeatedly for about a second, and the
output reports mean ± standard deviation together with some system information:

```shell
pixi run aoc{{ year }} -t
```

## Development

```shell
pixi run test       # pytest
pixi run lint       # ruff
pixi run typecheck  # ty
```

## Layout

- `src/aoc{{ year }}/taskNN.py`: one module per day. Solvers register themselves with
  `@solution1(day)` / `@solution2(day)` from `aoc_lib.registry`.
- `src/aoc{{ year }}/__init__.py`: generated by the template. It imports all task modules so
  every solver is registered. Don't edit it by hand.
- Inputs and examples are read from [`../inputs`](../inputs) and [`../examples`](../examples).
